Low-poly human — complete step-by-step

We'll use:

Cubes for torso, arms, and legs
UV Sphere for the head
Cylinders for simple hands/feet if needed
No complicated sculpting
0. Start

Open Blender → select the default cube.

Press A → X → Enter to delete everything.

1. Torso

Shift + A → Mesh → Cube

Press N → Item → Transform.

Set:

Location

X: 0
Y: 0
Z: 2.8

Rotation

X: 0°
Y: 0°
Z: 0°

Scale

X: 1.0
Y: 0.55
Z: 1.4

This is the main body.

2. Head

Shift + A → Mesh → UV Sphere

Set:

Location

X: 0
Y: 0
Z: 4.8

Rotation

X: 0°
Y: 0°
Z: 0°

Scale

X: 0.75
Y: 0.75
Z: 0.75

For a more low-poly appearance:

Object → Shade Auto Smooth
or right-click → Shade Smooth.

3. Left arm

Shift + A → Mesh → Cube

Set:

Location

X: -1.25
Y: 0
Z: 2.8

Rotation

X: 0°
Y: 0°
Z: 0°

Scale

X: 0.35
Y: 0.35
Z: 1.2

This gives you a simple straight arm.

4. Right arm

Select the left arm.

Press:

Shift + D → X → 2.5 → Enter

Now you have the second arm.

Its location becomes:

X: 1.25
Y: 0
Z: 2.8

Everything else stays the same.

5. Left leg

Shift + A → Mesh → Cube

Set:

Location

X: -0.5
Y: 0
Z: 0.3

Rotation

X: 0°
Y: 0°
Z: 0°

Scale

X: 0.45
Y: 0.45
Z: 1.3
6. Right leg

Select the left leg.

Shift + D → X → 1 → Enter

Right leg:

X: 0.5
Y: 0
Z: 0.3

Scale remains:

X: 0.45
Y: 0.45
Z: 1.3
7. Hands

For a very simple figure, you can use small spheres.

Left hand

Shift + A → Mesh → UV Sphere

Set:

Location X: -1.25
Y: 0
Z: 1.35
Scale: 0.4, 0.4, 0.4
Right hand

Duplicate:

Shift + D → X → 2.5 → Enter

8. Feet

Use cubes.

Left foot

Shift + A → Mesh → Cube

Set:

Location

X: -0.5
Y: -0.25
Z: -1.05

Scale

X: 0.5
Y: 0.8
Z: 0.3
Right foot

Duplicate:

Shift + D → X → 1 → Enter

9. Add a ground

Shift + A → Mesh → Plane

Set:

Location

X: 0
Y: 0
Z: -1.35

Scale

X: 4
Y: 4
Z: 4

Now the character stands on a floor.

10. Give it a simple material

Select the body.

Go to Material Properties → New.

Choose a simple color.

You can give:

Body → one color
Head → same color
Arms → same color
Legs → same color
Shoes → darker color

Then use Material Preview (Z → M) to see the colors.


11. now export from blender
click on file --> click on export --> select .fbx --> give your file a name.

12. Open Unity 
go to the asset window --> right click -->import new asset --> select your blender .fbx file
drag it to the scene and drop in scene view.

















